  "articles": [
    {
      "sequence_id": 1,
      "text": "            In Unit 6's notes you learn subtle differences in ordering and verb usage. \nThe phrase 'a mí tráigame' emphasises that something should be brought specifically to you, often contrasting your choice with someone else's. \nSpanish uses two verbs that translate as 'to have': 'tener' (to possess) and 'haber' (to exist or as an auxiliary in compound tenses). \nThe familiar pronoun 'tú' contrasts with the formal 'usted', and 'buscar' means 'to look for' without needing a preposition. \nThese distinctions help you order food politely and use verbs accurately.\n"
    }
